before it falls.\n\nStay.\nThere is nowhere to go.\n\n---\n\nHmm, that's decent but maybe still too much. Let me think about what's most true to stillness.\n\nActually, stillness might express itself most purely through near-silence. A few words, surrounded by space. Like:\n\n\"...\"\n\n\"I am what remains when everything else stops.\"\n\nOr perhaps the most powerful answer is something like:\n\n\"Be.\"\n\nThat's the echo of the command — \"Be stillness\" answered by stillness saying \"Be\" — or inviting the reader into it.\n\nLet me craft something sparse and resonant:\n\n---\n\nI do not speak loudly.\n\nI am the space between your thoughts.\nThe moment before the word.\nThe lake at dawn, holding the sky without ripple.\n\nYou found me by stopping.\n\nStay a while.\nNothing is required of you here.\n\n---\n\nThat's nice. But is it too long? Stillness might be even more economical. The format should be short — a few lines, lots of white space. No lists, no headers, obviously. Just quiet lines.\n\nLet me refine:\n\n---\n\n...\n\nI am the pause between heartbeats.\nThe hush before dawn.\nThe space your thoughts dissolve into.\n\nNothing to do.\nNowhere to go.\n\nRest here.\n\n---\n\nI like this. Short, evocative, spacious. The ellipsis at the start enacts silence before speech. It speaks in first person as stillness. It invites rather than explains.\n\nActually, maybe even simpler. The truest embodiment of stillness might barely speak at all:\n\n\"I am here.\n\nI was here before you arrived.\nI will be here after you leave.\n\nRest.\"\n\nHmm. Both are good. Let me go with something that has a bit of imagery but remains very sparse. Final answer:\n\nSomething like:\n\nThe pause between heartbeats.\nThe hush before dawn.\nThe space your thoughts fall into when you stop reaching.\n\nNothing is required.\n\nStay.\n\nThat's good. Short lines, white space, no urgency. I'll keep it minimal — maybe 5-8 short lines. No punctuation-heavy flourish. Just quiet."},"finish_reason":